  "retained_invariants": [
    "config required-argument unwrap follows validate_directive option metadata; optional arguments retain Option branches.",
    "hex digit unwraps follow whole-group ASCII-hex checks; chunks are at most two bytes, also bounding u8 accumulation. EDNS UTF8 unwrap follows that same ASCII proof.",
    "parse_rules pop follows slice.split, which yields at least one field even for an empty tail. Relay repeated address parse uses identical immutable bytes after Some guard.",
    "NameDomains stores only validated typed selectors and the immutable interface snapshot. Address lookup does not perform I/O or introduce a runtime configuration failure.",
    "loader line starts Some(0) and is never cleared; successful Stdio::piped spawn owns exactly one ChildStdout before take.",
    "netlink fixed-width unwraps follow complete-header/attribute bounds checks or exact family payload lengths. No test-only unwrap was mechanically removed.",
    "InterfaceCollector control fd exists throughout active states: only successful begin enters them; finish drops control and returns Idle.",
    "getifaddrs pointers remain inside an exclusively owned libc list; nullable addresses/names are checked, family determines sockaddr layout, and copied results outlive the single freeifaddrs guard.",
    "resolver getaddrinfo list is exclusively owned and address slices are bounded by family and ai_addrlen. Supported deployment musl/Darwin gai_strerror strings are static and non-null for their error codes.",
    "resolver fork is restricted to the documented single-thread owner; child owns copied fd table, keeps writer and calls _exit. Cancel/reap target only the positive unreaped owned child.",
    "Linux64 sockaddr_nl/ifreq/msghdr/iovec storage is initialized, aligned, live throughout syscall; successful fresh descriptors transfer into one OwnedFd/File.",
    "hosts forward/reverse indices are private and relocated on UID removal. Rotation last unwrap is inside positions.len()>1 and indices refer to current entries.",
    "host directory source IDs remain attached to literal per-directory paths including deletion/recreation. Initial scan follows regular symlinks but ignores nonregular files; runtime jobs own open fds across rename and never borrow path-derived memory."
  ],
